# The root .gitignore excludes `.env.*` so no real environment file is ever
# committed. `.env.example` holds no values, only the names and where they come
# from, and it is the only place those names are written down — so it has to be
# in the repo. A deeper .gitignore wins, which is why this exception lives here
# rather than loosening the root rule for the whole tree.
!.env.example
